# =============================================================================
# Network-layer allowlist for the AIVS edge listener.
#
# Copy to deploy/nginx/allowlist.conf, edit for your deployment, and point the
# proxy's bind mount at it in docker-compose.yml. The file shipped here is
# mounted by default so that a fresh checkout starts, but a production ISAC
# deployment MUST replace it -- see "Choosing a mode" below.
#
#   cp deploy/nginx/allowlist.conf.example deploy/nginx/allowlist.conf
#   # then in docker-compose.yml change the proxy mount to:
#   #   ./deploy/nginx/allowlist.conf:/etc/nginx/allowlist.conf:ro
#
# WHAT THIS IS AND IS NOT
#
# This is defence in depth, not the access control. Mutual TLS is the access
# control: a member is authenticated by a certificate this CA issued, and
# authorised against the AIVS database. Source addresses are spoofable at the
# network layer, change when a member re-homes, and are useless for a member
# behind carrier NAT.
#
# What an allowlist genuinely buys is a smaller attack surface for anything
# that happens BEFORE certificate verification: the TLS stack itself, nginx's
# HTTP parsing, and the connection-flood limits. A vulnerability in any of
# those is reachable pre-authentication by anyone who can open a socket. That
# is worth an IP restriction even though it is worth little on its own.
#
# The directives come from ngx_http_access_module. Rules are evaluated in
# order and the first match wins, so `deny all` must be last.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CHOOSING A MODE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Mode A -- WireGuard overlay (RECOMMENDED, and the default for the
# Aequora-managed hosting mode).
#
#   Every member peers into a WireGuard overlay (see deploy/wireguard/) and the
#   AIVS listener is published only on the overlay interface. Members reach
#   AIVS at its overlay address; nothing is exposed to the public internet at
#   all. In that topology $remote_addr is always an overlay address, this file
#   allows exactly the overlay subnet, and per-member revocation is done by
#   removing the peer -- which takes effect immediately and does not depend on
#   a member's public address staying put.
#
#   With WireGuard in front, the two controls compose: WireGuard authenticates
#   the *host*, mTLS authenticates the *organisation*, and neither substitutes
#   for the other.
#
#       allow 10.88.0.0/24;   # the AIVS WireGuard overlay
#       deny  all;
#
# Mode B -- public listener with a member CIDR allowlist.
#
#   For an ISAC that must accept connections from member networks directly.
#   Enumerate each member's egress ranges, one `allow` per line, with the
#   organisation slug in a comment so the list can be reconciled against the
#   AIVS organisation table during access reviews. Expect churn: an out-of-date
#   entry here causes a member outage that looks like a TLS problem.
#
# Mode C -- no network restriction (`allow all;`).
#
#   Only defensible when a layer in front of nginx already performs the same
#   filtering (a cloud load balancer security group, a corporate WAF). Write
#   down which layer, so that the next reviewer does not find an unexplained
#   `allow all` and assume the control was simply forgotten.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A DOCKER CAVEAT WORTH TESTING
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# $remote_addr must be the real client address for any of this to mean
# anything. With Docker's default iptables DNAT the source address is
# preserved, but connections arriving via the userland proxy (docker-proxy,
# used for loopback publishing on some platforms) are rewritten to the bridge
# gateway address. If that happens, every client appears to come from
# 172.x.0.1 and this file silently allows everyone.
#
# Verify after deployment, from outside the host:
#     curl --cert member.crt --key member.key https://aivs.example.org/healthz
# then confirm the "remote_addr" field in the proxy's JSON access log is the
# real source address and not a bridge gateway.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DEFAULT SHIPPED BELOW
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Private and overlay ranges only. This is a deliberate middle ground: a fresh
# checkout works for local and VPN-fronted deployments, and a deployment
# exposed directly to the internet is blocked until an operator makes a
# conscious decision. It is NOT a production allowlist for Mode B.
# =============================================================================

# WireGuard overlay used by deploy/wireguard/wg0.conf.example.
allow  10.88.0.0/24;

# RFC 1918 / RFC 4193 ranges, for ISAC-internal and VPN-fronted deployments.
allow  10.0.0.0/8;
allow  172.16.0.0/12;
allow  192.168.0.0/16;
allow  fc00::/7;

# Loopback, for on-host smoke tests.
allow  127.0.0.1/32;
allow  ::1/128;

# Everything else. Keep this last; the first matching rule wins.
deny   all;
